1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,555
This video is about one fact. Every regular language has exactly one minimal D F A, up to the names of the states.

2
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:24,770
We use a map between two machines, called a homomorphism. It shows that two machines agree. Then we show why the minimal D F A is the smallest one. Part 2 uses these facts to decide if two regexes are the same.

3
00:00:25,220 --> 00:00:29,913
So far we have the full pipeline. The templates give an N F A.

4
00:00:30,363 --> 00:00:33,265
The subset construction gives a D F A.

5
00:00:33,715 --> 00:00:37,064
Moore's algorithm gives the minimal D F A.

6
00:00:37,514 --> 00:00:46,367
Now a new question. Two different D F As accept the same language. How are they related? Do they give the same minimal machine?

7
00:00:46,817 --> 00:00:58,465
First, homomorphisms. A homomorphism is a map from the states of one D F A to the states of another. It proves that the two machines accept the same strings.

8
00:00:58,915 --> 00:01:05,721
Second, why the minimal D F A is the smallest. No D F A for the language can have fewer states.

9
00:01:06,171 --> 00:01:12,549
Third, deciding if two regexes are the same, and the cost of each step. That is part 2.

10
00:01:12,999 --> 00:01:19,847
At the top is our D F A for a or a b star, from the subset construction. It has four states.

11
00:01:20,297 --> 00:01:21,769
B and C accept.

12
00:01:22,219 --> 00:01:23,947
D is the error state.

13
00:01:24,397 --> 00:01:32,909
Below is the minimal machine from Moore's algorithm. Moore merged B and C into one state, B C. It has three states.

14
00:01:33,359 --> 00:01:40,293
The dashed arrows are a map. We call it h. Each state of our D F A goes to the state that it became.

15
00:01:40,743 --> 00:01:42,897
A goes to A. D goes to D.

16
00:01:43,347 --> 00:01:45,310
B and C both go to B C.

17
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:53,099
Now compare the arrows of the two machines. Take one arrow at the top, and follow h from both of its ends.

18
00:01:53,549 --> 00:01:55,767
B reads b and goes to C.

19
00:01:56,217 --> 00:01:59,929
h sends B to B C, and h sends C to B C.

20
00:02:00,379 --> 00:02:07,206
Below, B C reads b and goes to B C. So the step is still there. It is the loop on B C.

21
00:02:07,656 --> 00:02:12,477
Another arrow. C reads a and goes to D. Which step is that below?

22
00:02:12,927 --> 00:02:15,807
h sends C to B C, and D to D.

23
00:02:16,257 --> 00:02:21,889
Below, B C reads a and goes to D. Again the arrow is there, with the same letter.

24
00:02:22,339 --> 00:02:34,926
The top machine has four states and two letters, so eight transitions. Check all eight, and you find the same result. Every arrow up top lands on an arrow below with the same letter.

25
00:02:35,376 --> 00:02:40,752
Now run a string on both machines. Take a b b. Where does each machine end?

26
00:02:41,202 --> 00:02:44,487
Both machines start in A. And h of A is A.

27
00:02:44,937 --> 00:02:50,548
Read a. The top goes to B. The machine below goes to B C, which is h of B.

28
00:02:50,998 --> 00:02:56,950
Read b. The top goes to C. Below, B C loops to B C, which is h of C.

29
00:02:57,400 --> 00:03:03,011
Read the last b. The top stays in C, and the machine below stays in B C.

30
00:03:03,461 --> 00:03:09,391
Both end states accept. At each step, the state below is h of the state at the top.

31
00:03:09,841 --> 00:03:16,369
Here are the three things that we saw in the picture. First, the start state goes to the start state.

32
00:03:16,819 --> 00:03:20,873
Second, every step up top is a step below, with the same letter.

33
00:03:21,323 --> 00:03:24,053
Go right on x, and then down with h.

34
00:03:24,503 --> 00:03:32,567
Or go down with h, and then right on x. Both routes arrive at the same state, h of delta of q and x.

35
00:03:33,017 --> 00:03:39,268
Third, accepting states go to accepting states, and rejecting states go to rejecting states.

36
00:03:39,718 --> 00:03:52,774
So for every string, the run below is h of the run up top, step by step. One run ends in an accepting state exactly when the other does. The two machines accept the same strings.

37
00:03:53,224 --> 00:03:57,085
A map with these three properties is called a homomorphism.

38
00:03:57,535 --> 00:04:10,506
Now the same three lines, formally. M and M prime are two D F As over the same alphabet. A homomorphism h is a map from the states of M to the states of M prime.

39
00:04:10,956 --> 00:04:17,015
Line 1. h of the start state q zero is the start state q zero prime.

40
00:04:17,465 --> 00:04:28,771
Line 2. For every state q and letter x, h of delta of q and x equals delta prime of h of q and x. Reading a letter commutes with h.

41
00:04:29,221 --> 00:04:34,213
Line 3. q is accepting if and only if h of q is accepting.

42
00:04:34,663 --> 00:04:45,010
The consequence comes by induction on the length of the string w. Line 1 starts the two runs together: q zero goes to h of q zero.

43
00:04:45,460 --> 00:04:48,852
Line 2 keeps the two runs together for each letter.

44
00:04:49,302 --> 00:04:56,171
So h of the end state at the top is the end state below. Line 3 then gives the same answer.

45
00:04:56,621 --> 00:05:02,915
So the two machines have the same language. A homomorphism is a proof that two machines agree.

46
00:05:03,365 --> 00:05:17,039
You can check a homomorphism by hand with a table. Line 2 must hold for every state and every letter. Four states and two letters give eight rows. In each row, compare the two blue columns.

47
00:05:17,489 --> 00:05:25,511
A on a. A goes to B, and h of B is B C. Below, h of A is A, and A on a goes to B C. Same.

48
00:05:25,961 --> 00:05:32,190
A on b. A goes to D, and h of D is D. Below, A on b goes to D. Same.

49
00:05:32,640 --> 00:05:40,384
B on a. B goes to D, which maps to D. Below, h of B is B C, and B C on a goes to D.

50
00:05:40,834 --> 00:05:47,341
B on b. B goes to C, which maps to B C. Below, B C on b goes to B C.

51
00:05:47,791 --> 00:05:56,900
State C gives the same two rows as B, because C also maps to B C. C on a goes to D, and C on b goes to C.

52
00:05:57,350 --> 00:06:04,561
State D. D goes to D on both letters, and below, D also goes to D. All eight rows agree.

53
00:06:05,011 --> 00:06:18,984
Now lines 1 and 3. h of A is A, the start state. B and C accept and map to B C, which accepts. A and D reject and map to A and D, which reject. So h is a homomorphism.

54
00:06:19,434 --> 00:06:25,279
Here is another D F A for a or a b star. Its states are P, Q, R and S.

55
00:06:25,729 --> 00:06:27,372
Q and R accept.

56
00:06:27,822 --> 00:06:29,486
S is the error state.

57
00:06:29,936 --> 00:06:37,211
The shape is different. From R, the letter b goes back to Q. In our first D F A, C looped to itself.

58
00:06:37,661 --> 00:06:41,970
Now take the map P to A, Q and R to B C, and S to D.

59
00:06:42,420 --> 00:06:45,897
It maps this machine onto the same minimal machine.

60
00:06:46,347 --> 00:06:56,417
The slides say that every D F A for the language does this. If you minimize two of them, you get the same picture. Only the names of the states are different.

61
00:06:56,867 --> 00:07:02,413
Check the second map with the same table. Eight rows again, one per state and letter.

62
00:07:02,863 --> 00:07:14,298
P. On a, P goes to Q, which maps to B C. Below, A on a goes to B C. On b, P goes to S, which maps to D. Below, A on b goes to D.

63
00:07:14,748 --> 00:07:25,607
Q. On a, Q goes to S, so D. Below, B C on a goes to D. On b, Q goes to R, which maps to B C. Below, B C loops on b.

64
00:07:26,057 --> 00:07:36,041
R. On a, R goes to S, so D. On b, R goes back to Q, and Q maps to B C. The loop on B C covers this arrow too.

65
00:07:36,491 --> 00:07:40,800
S goes to S on both letters, and below, D goes to D.

66
00:07:41,250 --> 00:07:55,629
P maps to the start state A. Q and R accept, and their image B C accepts. P and S reject, and so do A and D. So this map is also a homomorphism onto the same minimal machine.

67
00:07:56,079 --> 00:08:06,063
The slides add two conditions on the input D F A. Total means that every state has an arrow on every letter, so the error state is drawn.

68
00:08:06,513 --> 00:08:10,779
Trim means that every state is reached from the start by some string.

69
00:08:11,229 --> 00:08:15,027
Here the machine has no sink. Q has no arrow on a.

70
00:08:15,477 --> 00:08:22,047
So delta of Q and a does not exist, and line 2 has no left side for this state and letter.

71
00:08:22,497 --> 00:08:25,911
Here the machine has a state U that no string reaches.

72
00:08:26,361 --> 00:08:33,315
The map gets a state from a string that reaches it. No string reaches U, so h has no rule for U.

73
00:08:33,765 --> 00:08:43,600
So the statement is for trim, total D F As. Each of them maps onto the minimal machine. This is one more reason to draw the error state.

74
00:08:44,050 --> 00:08:52,263
The minimal machine for a or a b star has three states. Could some other D F A for the language get away with two states?

75
00:08:52,713 --> 00:08:59,711
Pick one string that reaches each state of the minimal machine. The empty string, epsilon, reaches A.

76
00:09:00,161 --> 00:09:02,017
The string a reaches B C.

77
00:09:02,467 --> 00:09:04,173
The string b reaches D.

78
00:09:04,623 --> 00:09:12,922
Now compare them in pairs. Epsilon and a. Add the empty suffix. Then a is in the language, and epsilon is not.

79
00:09:13,372 --> 00:09:19,687
Epsilon and b. Add the suffix a. Then the string a is in the language, and b a is not.

80
00:09:20,137 --> 00:09:24,851
a and b. Add the empty suffix. a is in the language, and b is not.

81
00:09:25,301 --> 00:09:29,397
So every two of the three strings are told apart by some suffix.

82
00:09:29,847 --> 00:09:35,778
Any D F A for a or a b star reads epsilon, a and b from its start state.

83
00:09:36,228 --> 00:09:40,303
Suppose that a D F A for the language has only two states.

84
00:09:40,753 --> 00:09:47,515
Three strings and two states. So two of the strings end in the same state. Say epsilon and a.

85
00:09:47,965 --> 00:09:58,675
After that state, every suffix does the same thing for both strings. The suffix leads to one end state. So the machine accepts both or rejects both.

86
00:09:59,125 --> 00:10:09,471
But with the empty suffix, a is in the language, and epsilon is not. That is a contradiction. The same thing happens for each pair of the three strings.

87
00:10:09,921 --> 00:10:18,839
So the three strings end in three different states. Every D F A for the language has at least three states. Ours has exactly three.

88
00:10:19,289 --> 00:10:27,715
A python check also tried all 64 two-state D F As over a and b. None of them accepts this language.

89
00:10:28,165 --> 00:10:40,091
The same argument works for any machine that minimization returns. Moore splits two states only when a suffix forces it. So every pair of its states has a separating suffix.

90
00:10:40,541 --> 00:10:59,271
The last slides of the lecture go deeper. They are background for the facts above. Two states p and q are equivalent if no string is accepted from one and rejected from the other. The block of q is the set of states equivalent to q. Here the block of B is B and C.

91
00:10:59,721 --> 00:11:10,687
This relation is a congruence. If p and q are equivalent, then on each letter they go to equivalent states. On a, B and C both go to D.

92
00:11:11,137 --> 00:11:17,025
On b, B goes to C and C goes to C. Both targets are in the block B C.

93
00:11:17,475 --> 00:11:24,707
The reason: if a suffix w separated the two targets, then x w would separate p and q.

94
00:11:25,157 --> 00:11:30,853
So we can build the quotient machine. Its states are the blocks: A, B C and D.

95
00:11:31,303 --> 00:11:34,865
Its start state is the block of the start state, A.

96
00:11:35,315 --> 00:11:46,409
On letter x, the block of q goes to the block of delta of q and x. The choice of q in the block does not matter, because the relation is a congruence.

97
00:11:46,859 --> 00:11:54,645
The accepting states are the blocks inside F. Here that is B C. This is exactly the minimal machine below.

98
00:11:55,095 --> 00:12:04,205
The theorem. Let M be trim and total. The map that sends each state to its block is a surjective homomorphism onto the quotient.

99
00:12:04,655 --> 00:12:10,628
Surjective means that every state of the quotient is hit, because every block is non-empty.

100
00:12:11,078 --> 00:12:29,382
And no D F A for the language has fewer states than the quotient. The algorithm splits only when a suffix forces it, so any D F A for the language must keep those states apart too. The slides name Moore, 1956, and the Myhill Nerode theorem.

101
00:12:29,832 --> 00:12:39,773
The corollary. If two machines have the same language, then their quotients are isomorphic. They are the same machine after a renaming of states.

102
00:12:40,223 --> 00:12:47,925
The renaming is unique, because in a trim machine every state is reached by some string, so the map has no choices.

103
00:12:48,375 --> 00:13:02,284
So the minimal D F A is not just a smallest one. It is the smallest one, up to the names of the states. The homomorphisms also tell you which state of one machine does the job of which state of the other.

104
00:13:02,734 --> 00:13:16,281
To summarize. A homomorphism maps the states of one D F A to the states of another. The start goes to the start, each step goes to a step with the same letter, and accepting goes to accepting.

105
00:13:16,731 --> 00:13:25,328
A homomorphism proves that two D F As accept the same strings. You can check one with a table of all states and letters.

106
00:13:25,778 --> 00:13:31,687
Every trim, total D F A for a language maps onto the same minimal D F A.

107
00:13:32,137 --> 00:13:41,481
The minimal D F A is the smallest. Strings that reach different states are told apart by some suffix, so no machine can merge them.

108
00:13:41,931 --> 00:13:52,598
And the minimal D F A is unique up to the names of its states. In part 2 we use this fact to decide if two regexes describe the same language.
